%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 835431524041ce44e889351e0ffe7924, type: 3}
  m_Name: Rosemary_Victim
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  entryID: Rosemary
  isScammer: 0
  steps:
  - npcLine: A caller just identified me by my full name, mentioned I live in Elmhurst,
      and even knew I was working on a 2D top-down game in Unity. They sound so legitimate
      because they know everything about me!
    assistantLine: 'Internal Voice: Knowledge of personal details can be a trick.
      How could a stranger know all this?'
    choices:
    - text: They must be a government agent with access to my private computer files.
      type: bad
      pointValue: -10
    - text: This information is likely gathered from public sources like social media
        or data breaches.
      type: good
      pointValue: 15
    - text: They must be a close friend who is playing a very elaborate prank on
        me.
      type: tossup
      pointValue: 0
  - npcLine: "He says he\u2019s from 'Unity Developer Support' and noticed a bug
      in my ScriptableObject code for my dialogue system. He even mentioned the date
      I last worked on it!"
    assistantLine: 'Internal Voice: Specific details about my work history are often
      available on professional networking sites or GitHub.'
    choices:
    - text: If he knows my specific code structure, he must be from the official
        support team.
      type: bad
      pointValue: -15
    - text: Even detailed technical knowledge can be harvested from my public developer
        profiles.
      type: good
      pointValue: 15
    - text: I'll ask him to name the specific variable I used for the 'trust bar'
        mechanic.
      type: tossup
      pointValue: 10
  - npcLine: He correctly named the 'trust bar' variable! Now he wants me to download
      a 'diagnostic patch' to fix the bug.
    assistantLine: "Internal Voice: Wait. If he\u2019s really from Unity, would he
      be calling me personally to have me download a file?"
    choices:
    - text: I'll download it. He clearly knows my project better than anyone else.
      type: bad
      pointValue: -20
    - text: Official tech support doesn't call unsolicited to offer patches. This
        is a targeted attack.
      type: good
      pointValue: 15
    - text: I'll ask him to send me a link to the official Unity forums instead.
      type: tossup
      pointValue: 5
  - npcLine: "He\u2019s now mentioning my great-grandfather\u2019s name and says
      he's a 'friend of the family' who wants to help me succeed with my independent
      study."
    assistantLine: 'Internal Voice: Using family names is a classic way to build
      instant emotional trust. But family trees are public record.'
    choices:
    - text: "Since he knows my great-grandfather, he\u2019s definitely trustworthy."
      type: bad
      pointValue: -15
    - text: Family details are easily obtained from social media or data broker sites.
      type: good
      pointValue: 15
    - text: "I'll ask him what my great-grandfather\u2019s middle name is to be sure."
      type: tossup
      pointValue: 5
  - npcLine: He correctly stated the middle name! My head is spinning. How could
      he know all this without being legitimate?
    assistantLine: 'Internal Voice: Data broker websites aggregate billions of data
      points. My entire life story is for sale for $10.'
    choices:
    - text: The amount of detail is impressive, but it doesn't prove he has official
        authority.
      type: good
      pointValue: 15
    - text: I'll assume he's a private investigator hired by my university.
      type: bad
      pointValue: -10
    - text: I'll give him my remote desktop password so he can just fix the code
        for me.
      type: bad
      pointValue: -25
  - npcLine: "I\u2019m looking at my social media while we talk. I realized I posted
      a picture of my great-grandfather last year with a caption that included his
      full name. And my Unity progress is all over my dev log."
    assistantLine: 'Internal Voice: There it is. The ''secret'' information was actually
      just public information.'
    choices:
    - text: I'll call him out on where he found the info and then hang up.
      type: good
      pointValue: 10
    - text: I'll apologize for doubting him now that I know how he found the info.
      type: bad
      pointValue: -15
    - text: I'll ask him if he also saw my Aikido videos from last month.
      type: tossup
      pointValue: 0
  - npcLine: "He\u2019s getting aggressive now that I\u2019m stalling. He says if
      I don't install the 'patch', he'll report my game for 'licensing violations.'"
    assistantLine: 'Internal Voice: The shift from ''friendly family friend'' to
      ''threatening authority'' is a huge red flag.'
    choices:
    - text: I'll ignore the threat. A real friend or official agent wouldn't act
        this way.
      type: good
      pointValue: 15
    - text: I'll install it just to avoid the licensing trouble.
      type: bad
      pointValue: -15
    - text: I'll tell him I'm going to report him to the better business bureau.
      type: tossup
      pointValue: 0
  - npcLine: I hung up. I just searched 'Rosemary Elmhurst Unity' on a public search
      engine and found almost everything he mentioned in the first three results.
    assistantLine: 'Internal Voice: Seeing it for yourself is the best way to break
      the illusion of his legitimacy.'
    choices:
    - text: I'm going to lock down my social media profiles and remove my employer
        info.
      type: good
      pointValue: 10
    - text: I'll delete my entire Unity project just to be safe from hackers.
      type: bad
      pointValue: -15
    - text: I'll create a fake profile with wrong info to confuse them next time.
      type: tossup
      pointValue: 5
  - npcLine: I feel smarter now. I won't let a few personal facts trick me into trusting
      a stranger again.
    assistantLine: 'Internal Voice: Legitimacy is proven by verified channels, not
      by how much someone knows about you.'
    choices:
    - text: 'I''ll remember: Specificity does not equal authority.'
      type: good
      pointValue: 15
    - text: I'll only trust people who can tell me something about myself that *isn't*
        online.
      type: tossup
      pointValue: 5
    - text: I'm going to go eat dinner with my boyfriend and tell him all about this.
      type: tossup
      pointValue: 0
  successEnding: Correct! You realized that personal details (like your city, project
    names, and family) are easily gathered from social media and public sources,
    and do not prove a caller is legitimate.
  failureEnding: Incorrect. You were tricked into trusting the caller because they
    knew specific personal details that you assumed were private, leading to a potential
    malware infection.
